**Read:** SI Brochure chapters 2–5, initially concentrating on the unit tables and writing conventions. Read Math for Trades, Volume 2, Understanding and Working with Units completely. Revisit Mahajan's The Art of Insight, Dimensions, after the first worked conversions.

A dimension describes the kind of physical dependence, such as length L, mass M, or time T. A unit is a chosen reference used to express a value. Speed has dimension LT^−1 whether reported in m/s or km/h. Dimensional equality is a necessary check on a physical equation, not proof that the model is true.

For a cylindrical container V = πr²h has dimension L³. If a proposed formula adds r² to h, its terms cannot represent the same physical quantity. A ratio may have unit one while still having a specific meaning: mass fraction, strain, and a count ratio should retain informative names. Angle and frequency conventions also need their quantity names, not only the algebra of dimensions.

Pressure is force per area, with SI unit Pa = N/m²; energy has unit J = N m; power has unit W = J/s. Torque and energy share dimensions but answer different questions. A dimensional check cannot turn one into the other.

**Work:** derive the dimensions of density, volumetric flow, mass flow, energy per mass, and energy per manufactured item. Check Q = Av for volumetric flow and m = ρV for mass. Construct one dimensionally valid but physically false equation and explain what empirical or theoretical evidence would be needed beyond matching units. Keep this distinction when later reading process models.

